    "body": "<p>A coastal soundscape is where land, sea, and human activity meet in sound. Waves break with a rhythmic surge and crash, the period set by swell and shore shape, while wind adds a steady hiss and gulls, terns, and waders call over the water. Below the surface, the coastal ocean holds snapping shrimp, fish choruses, and, in season, whale calls, so the coast has two soundscapes, one above water and one below, linked by the shore itself. Anthrophony is rarely far away: harbours, boats, beach visitors, and coastal roads add engine and voice noise, and in busy coasts the boat layer can dominate the underwater soundscape. Tides shape the whole pattern: a high tide brings waves closer and louder, a low tide exposes mudflats where waders feed and call, and the twice-daily cycle gives coastal soundscapes a rhythm no inland place matches.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: coastal soundscape studies measure wave-noise spectra against tide and swell, document seabird colonies acoustically, and use hydrophones to track fish choruses, shrimp snaps, and boat noise in the same waters. Tidal modulation of coastal sound levels is a measured effect, and boat-noise masking of fish communication is documented in harbour and reef studies.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if waves break every 8 seconds, the wave rate is 60 / 8 = 7.5 waves per minute, or 450 waves per hour. The demo prints 7.5 waves per minute and 450 waves per hour, plus the waves in a 6-hour tidal half-cycle, 450 x 6 = 2700 waves.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony; anthrophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Published coastal soundscape literature (wave noise, seabird colonies, harbour hydrophone studies); figures as published October 2026</p>",
